---
description: Bind NUnit decimal TestCase args with strings to avoid double conversion noise
alwaysApply: true
---

# Decimal TestCase values

NUnit attribute literals are `double`. Feeding them into `decimal` parameters is lossy—especially on .NET 11+, where double→decimal is correctly rounded and no longer looks like `12.37M`.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — expected becomes 12.369999…M; Round() returns 12.37M
[TestCase(12.366, 12.37, RoundingType.Rounding001)]
public void CanRound(decimal value, decimal expected, RoundingType type) { … }

// ✅ GOOD — NUnit parses exact decimals from strings
[TestCase("12.366", "12.37", RoundingType.Rounding001)]
public void CanRound(decimal value, decimal expected, RoundingType type) { … }
```

Prefer string TestCase args or `TestCaseSource` with `m` literals. Do not “fix” by loosening assertions with large tolerances unless the behavior under test is inherently approximate.
